---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Rawan Aladdin — For Canadian Blood Services"
  description="A targeted mini-portfolio: one brand system carried across social, web, print, events and video, and templates made for other people to keep using, by Rawan Aladdin, for the Graphic Designer/Multimedia Designer role in Integrated Marketing."
  bodyClass="cb-pitch"
  canonical="https://rawanaladdin.com/cbs"
  noindex
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cbs.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <main>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ HERO ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="hero" class="cb-section" style="min-height: 100vh; justify-content: flex-end; padding-bottom: 8rem;">
      <p class="cb-hero-label">For Canadian Blood Services — Graphic Designer/Multimedia Designer, Integrated Marketing — September 2026</p>
      <h1 class="cb-hero-h1">Hi Canadian Blood Services,<br>I'm Rawan.</h1>
      <p class="cb-hero-sub">
        A national campaign gets made once. Then it has to hold up on a clinic
        poster, a story announcing Saturday hours, and a flyer someone in donor
        relations edits on a Tuesday afternoon. I design brand systems and then
        apply them myself, and so the twentieth asset still reads as the same
        brand as the first.
      </p>
      <div class="cb-hero-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="cb-divider"></div>

    <!-- ══════════════════ THE LEDGER — channel by channel ══════════════════ -->
    <section id="ledger" class="cb-section">
      <p class="cb-section-label">Your posting, channel by channel</p>
      <h2 class="cb-h2">Where the work goes,<br>and where mine has gone.</h2>
      <p class="cb-lede">
        Your posting lists the channels plainly: social, web, marketing
        collateral, event and engagement materials, motion and video, and
        templates in editable formats. Below is the nearest real piece of mine
        for each, with a picture of it so the comparison can be checked.
      </p>
      <p class="cb-zoom-hint">Every image on this page opens full size — the thumbnails here are crops.</p>

      <div class="cb-ledger">

        <div class="cb-row">
          <p class="cb-row-k">Social</p>
          <p class="cb-row-v">
            <span class="cb-row-a">SyriaTek</span>
            A bilingual identity for an Arabic-language technology series, with
            the profile mark and the cover art each cut to their platform's own
            crop, and two vertical pilots subtitled in Arabic and English.
            <strong>1,556 unique viewers in fifteen days</strong>, roughly 92% of
            them from outside the account.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-thumb">
            <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-cover.webp" class="cb-contain" alt="The SyriaTek Facebook cover: the Arabic and Latin wordmarks centred above a rising chevron of circuit traces, with the Arabic tagline beneath in green." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-row">
          <p class="cb-row-k">Motion and video</p>
          <p class="cb-row-v">
            <span class="cb-row-a">SyriaTek, and the Feeds launch</span>
            Two pilots I presented, cut, subtitled and exported for Reels, and the
            looping motion that played on the Feeds launch screens. Before either,
            a year of photo and video post-production in Adobe Creative Cloud for a
            students' association.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-thumb">
            <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-timeline.webp" alt="The SyriaTek edit timeline in CapCut, showing the B-roll clips against two separate caption tracks." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-row">
          <p class="cb-row-k">Web</p>
          <p class="cb-row-v">
            <span class="cb-row-a">The Feeds marketing site, and SACABC</span>
            The Feeds site carries the same identity as the product and the
            printed pieces. Before it, a cultural nonprofit's site
            <strong>rebuilt in WordPress</strong>, with its web and social graphics
            made in Figma as one set.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-thumb">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-site-hero.webp" alt="The Feeds marketing site hero: the Feeds wordmark and mark over a grey topographic texture, with the line 'Your feeds. Your disk. Your loop.'" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-row">
          <p class="cb-row-k">Event and<br>engagement<br>materials</p>
          <p class="cb-row-v">
            <span class="cb-row-a">The Feeds launch booth</span>
            Printed table runners, die-cut stickers in four colourways, and two
            reels looping on the screens behind them — every piece cut from the
            same identity in the same week, for a room of people walking past.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-thumb">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-booth.webp" class="cb-crop-low" alt="The Feeds launch booth: printed table runners along the table edge, die-cut stickers laid out, and the product on screens behind." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-row">
          <p class="cb-row-k">Templates in<br>editable formats</p>
          <p class="cb-row-v">
            <span class="cb-row-a">Surface Fixer</span>
            For a surface repair business I designed the wordmark and then built
            an invoice template and a document template system in
            <strong>Google Docs and Sheets</strong>, so the owner produces branded
            paperwork on his own.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-thumb">
            <img src="/surfacefixer-cover.webp" alt="The Surface Fixer wordmark in heavy navy capitals, its X replaced by two crossed bandages, over a pattern of the same bandage mark on a navy-to-white gradient." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-row">
          <p class="cb-row-k">Working to brand<br>guidelines</p>
          <p class="cb-row-v">
            <span class="cb-row-a">The Feeds brand guidelines</span>
            Eleven pages I wrote for the engineer building the product, and then
            had to follow myself across the interface, the digest email, the site
            and the booth. Knowing what each rule costs the person who follows it
            is most of what makes a rulebook easy to keep.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-thumb">
            <img src="/feeds/guidelines-06.webp" alt="Feeds guidelines page 06: the five-colour palette with hex and RGB values, stated roles, and a usage-ratio bar." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p class="cb-note">
        Two things in your posting, stated plainly. The templates above were built
        in Google's tools, and Word and PowerPoint templates work on the same idea.
        And your posting names infographics: I have made them for coursework in
        nearly every class at BCIT, and this ledger is kept to work that left the
        classroom.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="cb-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═════════════════ MADE FOR SOMEBODY ELSE TO USE ═════════════════ -->
    <section id="system" class="cb-section">
      <p class="cb-section-label">Made for somebody else to use</p>
      <h2 class="cb-h2">The part of the job<br>that outlives the brief.</h2>
      <p class="cb-lede">
        Your posting asks for templated resources and a library that teams across
        the country can pull from. That work only succeeds when the person using
        it never has to ask the designer anything. Both of these were made for
        exactly that person.
      </p>

      <div class="cb-spec">
        <div><div class="cb-spec-n">11</div><div class="cb-spec-l">Pages</div></div>
        <div><div class="cb-spec-n">5</div><div class="cb-spec-l">Type steps</div></div>
        <div><div class="cb-spec-n">15</div><div class="cb-spec-l">Icons, one grammar</div></div>
        <div><div class="cb-spec-n">AA</div><div class="cb-spec-l">Contrast floor</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="cb-pages">
        <div class="cb-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-05.webp" alt="Feeds guidelines page 05: logo usage, with three approved lockup combinations and four don'ts." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="cb-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-06.webp" alt="Feeds guidelines page 06: the five-colour palette with hex and RGB values, stated roles, and a usage-ratio bar." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="cb-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-07.webp" alt="Feeds guidelines page 07: the five-step type scale and the variable font axes." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="cb-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-08.webp" alt="Feeds guidelines page 08: fifteen custom icons sharing one drawing grammar." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cb-caption">Four of the eleven Feeds guideline pages. The client was the engineer building the product.</p>

      <div class="cb-block">
        <p class="cb-block-n">Feeds</p>
        <h3>A rulebook written for one person in a hurry.</h3>
        <p>
          Every page had to answer a question the engineer would otherwise have to
          ask me. So the palette carries the share of a page each colour should
          take, the type scale names what each step is for, and the don'ts are
          drawn out as pictures. Then I followed it myself, across the product,
          the digest email, the marketing site and the launch booth.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cb-block">
        <p class="cb-block-n">Surface Fixer</p>
        <h3>Paperwork that stays on brand.</h3>
        <p>
          The identity came first: a heavy wordmark whose X is two crossed
          bandages, with the same mark repeating out into a background pattern.
          Then the everyday material — an invoice template and a document
          template system, built in Google Docs and Sheets — so the owner sends
          out branded paperwork without me in the room. The work is ongoing, and
          the site redesign is next.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cb-decision">
        <p class="cb-decision-k">What I would bring to your library</p>
        <p>
          A template is finished once someone else can fill it in correctly
          without calling the designer. That is the test I would hold every
          editable file to, and it is the same test the Feeds guidelines were
          written against.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="cb-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ FOOTAGE ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="footage" class="cb-section">
      <p class="cb-section-label">Video and motion</p>
      <h2 class="cb-h2">Footage in,<br>assets out.</h2>
      <p class="cb-lede">
        Much of this work arrives as footage somebody else shot, and leaves as a
        short cut exported for one particular feed. That is how both of these
        pilots were made.
      </p>

      <!-- The two pilots, playing. Each blockquote carries a real card as its
           contents — Instagram's script replaces it on success, and it stands
           on its own if the script is blocked. -->
      <div class="cb-ig-grid">

        <div class="cb-ig">
          <p class="cb-ig-k">Pilot one — Welcome</p>
          <blockquote
            class="instagram-media"
            data-instgrm-permalink="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbNR4HenYQQ/?utm_source=ig_embed"
            data-instgrm-version="14"
          >
            <div class="cb-ig-fallback">
              <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-frame-1.webp" alt="A frame from the SyriaTek Welcome pilot: the presenter on camera with Arabic and English subtitles stacked beneath." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
              <div class="cb-ig-fb-body">
                <p class="cb-ig-fb-t">SyriaTek — Welcome</p>
                <p class="cb-ig-fb-p">Vertical, 1080&nbsp;×&nbsp;1920, cut in CapCut and subtitled in Arabic and English.</p>
                <a href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbNR4HenYQQ/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">watch on instagram &#8599;</a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </blockquote>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-ig">
          <p class="cb-ig-k">Pilot two — Digital Skills</p>
          <blockquote
            class="instagram-media"
            data-instgrm-permalink="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbPvv6KhE3K/?utm_source=ig_embed"
            data-instgrm-version="14"
          >
            <div class="cb-ig-fallback">
              <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-broll-2.webp" alt="A B-roll frame from SyriaTek: a street in Syria at dusk, with shopfronts, a food stall and people walking past." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
              <div class="cb-ig-fb-body">
                <p class="cb-ig-fb-t">SyriaTek — Digital Skills</p>
                <p class="cb-ig-fb-p">Presenter footage cut against street B-roll, same format, same two caption tracks.</p>
                <a href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbPvv6KhE3K/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">watch on instagram &#8599;</a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </blockquote>
        </div>

      </div>
      <p class="cb-caption">
        Both published 25 July 2026 to instagram.com/syria.tek. Between them, 3,823
        views and 1,556 unique viewers in fifteen days, with roughly 92% of the reach
        coming from people who did not follow the account.
      </p>

      <div class="cb-block">
        <p class="cb-block-n">SyriaTek</p>
        <h3>Twenty-one clips from Syria, cut into two pilots.</h3>
        <p>
          SyriaTek is an Arabic-language technology series I designed and made: the
          bilingual identity and animated wordmark, a reusable script and shot-list
          guide, and both pilots, which I presented, cut vertical and subtitled in
          Arabic and English. My sister filmed the twenty-one B-roll clips on the
          street in Syria while she was there this summer, and I chose them and
          cut them in.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cb-media cb-media-narrow">
        <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-timeline.webp" alt="The SyriaTek edit timeline in CapCut, showing the B-roll clips against two separate caption tracks." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <p class="cb-caption">The edit, with the Arabic and English caption tracks running separately.</p>

      <div class="cb-block">
        <p class="cb-block-n">Muslim Students' Association, University of Toronto</p>
        <h3>An academic year of coverage, on a volunteer crew.</h3>
        <p>
          As Digital Media Director I ran a committee of volunteer shooters,
          assigned event coverage with the executive team, and handled the
          post-production in Adobe Creative Cloud. Briefing someone who is
          giving up their Saturday teaches you to be specific about the shot you
          actually need.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cb-decision">
        <p class="cb-decision-k">The decision I would bring up in an interview</p>
        <p>
          Writing the second script, I changed <bdi lang="ar">موصول</bdi>
          <span class="cb-tr">(mawsool)</span> to <bdi lang="ar">متصل</bdi>
          <span class="cb-tr">(muttasil)</span>. A dictionary gives both as
          &ldquo;connected&rdquo;. Only one of them is what people actually say
          about a phone or a network, and it was not the one I had written first.
          I cut the term for router and described the Wi-Fi device at home or at
          work. None of that shows in the finished video, and all of it is the
          reason it lands. Asking someone to give blood runs on the same instinct:
          the words that reach a reader are the ones already in their mouth.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="cb-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ ABOUT ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="about" class="cb-section" style="min-height: 80vh;">
      <p class="cb-section-label">About</p>
      <h2 class="cb-h2">Why this<br>one.</h2>
      <p class="cb-lede">
        The cause is personal to me. My family was in Syria during the war, and so
        I know how critical blood services are to the people living through it,
        and how life-changing a donation can be. Making that case to people across
        Canada is work I would be proud to do every day.
      </p>
      <p class="cb-lede">
        I'm in Vancouver, and the hybrid arrangement suits me. I hold a Bachelor
        of Creative Industries and a Diploma in Digital Design &amp; Development,
        both from BCIT, and I work in Arabic and English. A donor decides in a few
        seconds whether a poster is talking to them, and every asset I make for
        your team would be built for those seconds.
      </p>

      <div class="cb-cta-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/rawan-aladdin" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">linkedin &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="cb-footnote">
        Made for Canadian Blood Services Integrated Marketing, September 2026 — posting 9225.
      </p>
    </section>

  </main>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ LIGHTBOX ═══════════════════════════════
       Rawan asked for images that open full size. Every thumbnail in the ledger
       is object-fit: cover, so a reviewer was seeing a crop of the very artefact
       the row exists to evidence.

       Progressive enhancement on purpose: the buttons and the overlay are built
       here at runtime rather than shipped in the markup, so with JS blocked the
       page is exactly what it was before — every image still visible, nothing
       broken, no empty dialog in the DOM. The argument never depends on this.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Instagram's embed script. async, and nothing on the page waits on it:
       if it never arrives, each blockquote keeps the card it already contains. -->
  <script is:inline async src="https://www.instagram.com/embed.js"></script>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var imgs = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.cb-thumb img, .cb-media img'));
      if (!imgs.length) return;

      var lb = document.createElement('div');
      lb.className = 'cb-lb';
      lb.setAttribute('role', 'dialog');
      lb.setAttribute('aria-modal', 'true');
      lb.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Image viewer');
      lb.setAttribute('data-open', 'false');
      lb.innerHTML =
        '<div class="cb-lb-stage"><img class="cb-lb-img" alt=""></div>' +
        '<div class="cb-lb-foot"><p class="cb-lb-cap"></p><p class="cb-lb-count"></p></div>' +
        '<button type="button" class="cb-lb-close" aria-label="Close image viewer"></button>' +
        '<button type="button" class="cb-lb-prev" aria-label="Previous image"></button>' +
        '<button type="button" class="cb-lb-next" aria-label="Next image"></button>';
      document.body.appendChild(lb);

      var stage = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-stage');
      var foot = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-foot');
      var imgEl = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-img');
      var capEl = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-cap');
      var countEl = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-count');
      var closeBtn = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-close');
      var prevBtn = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-prev');
      var nextBtn = lb.querySelector('.cb-lb-next');
      var main = document.querySelector('main');

      var current = -1, lastFocus = null, savedBody = 0, savedDoc = 0;

      // Wrap each image in a real <button>, so this is reachable by keyboard and
      // announced as a control rather than being a click handler on a picture.
      var items = imgs.map(function (img, i) {
        var label = img.getAttribute('alt') || 'Image';
        var btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        btn.className = 'cb-zoom';
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'View full size: ' + label);
        img.parentNode.insertBefore(btn, img);
        btn.appendChild(img);
        btn.addEventListener('click', function () { open(i, btn); });
        // data-full points at a larger original where one exists; the page
        // itself only needs the smaller file.
        return {
          src: img.getAttribute('data-full') || img.getAttribute('src'),
          cap: label
        };
      });

      if (items.length < 2) { prevBtn.remove(); nextBtn.remove(); }

      function show(i) {
        current = (i + items.length) % items.length;
        imgEl.src = items[current].src;
        imgEl.alt = items[current].cap;
        capEl.textContent = items[current].cap;
        countEl.textContent = (current + 1) + ' / ' + items.length;
      }

      function open(i, trigger) {
        // Take the trigger explicitly. Relying on document.activeElement is
        // wrong for any click that does not focus its target.
        lastFocus = trigger || document.activeElement;
        // body is the scroll container on this site — html is overflow:hidden
        // and both are height:100%, so document.scrollingElement reports
        // documentElement and window.scrollY stays 0. Measured 2026-08-25.
        // Save both and restore both rather than picking one with ||.
        savedBody = document.body.scrollTop;
        savedDoc = document.documentElement.scrollTop;
        show(i);
        lb.setAttribute('data-open', 'true');
        document.body.classList.add('cb-lb-open');
        if (main && 'inert' in main) main.inert = true;
        // The dialog is visibility:hidden until this style change is recalculated,
        // and a hidden element cannot take focus. Flush layout, then focus, and
        // keep a frame in reserve in case the flush was not enough.
        void lb.offsetHeight;
        closeBtn.focus();
        if (document.activeElement !== closeBtn) {
          requestAnimationFrame(function () { closeBtn.focus(); });
        }
      }

      function close() {
        if (lb.getAttribute('data-open') !== 'true') return;
        lb.setAttribute('data-open', 'false');
        document.body.classList.remove('cb-lb-open');
        if (main && 'inert' in main) main.inert = false;
        // Restore only after the scroll lock is lifted, or it is a no-op.
        document.body.scrollTop = savedBody;
        document.documentElement.scrollTop = savedDoc;
        if (lastFocus && lastFocus.focus) lastFocus.focus();
      }

      closeBtn.addEventListener('click', close);
      prevBtn.addEventListener('click', function () { show(current - 1); });
      nextBtn.addEventListener('click', function () { show(current + 1); });

      // Clicking the surround closes; clicking the image itself does not.
      lb.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
        if (e.target === lb || e.target === stage || e.target === foot) close();
      });

      document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
        if (lb.getAttribute('data-open') !== 'true') return;
        if (e.key === 'Escape') { e.preventDefault(); close(); return; }
        if (items.length > 1 && e.key === 'ArrowRight') { e.preventDefault(); show(current + 1); return; }
        if (items.length > 1 && e.key === 'ArrowLeft') { e.preventDefault(); show(current - 1); return; }
        if (e.key === 'Tab') {
          // Keep focus inside the dialog. <main> is inert, but the browser
          // chrome and anything else in <body> is not.
          var f = [closeBtn, prevBtn, nextBtn].filter(function (b) { return b.isConnected; });
          var at = f.indexOf(document.activeElement);
          e.preventDefault();
          f[e.shiftKey ? (at <= 0 ? f.length - 1 : at - 1)
                       : (at === f.length - 1 || at === -1 ? 0 : at + 1)].focus();
        }
      });
    })();
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
